p.src:
Choose $\beta=
\begin{pmatrix}
0&1\\
1&0
\end{pmatrix}.$ Its square can be checked entry by entry: $\begin{aligned}
\beta^2
&=
\begin{pmatrix}
0&1\\
1&0
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
0&1\\
1&0
\end{pmatrix}\\
&=
\begin{pmatrix}
0\cdot0+1\cdot1&0\cdot1+1\cdot0\\
1\cdot0+0\cdot1&1\cdot1+0\cdot0
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
1&0\\
0&1
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}$ The order of multiplication matters: $\begin{aligned}
\alpha\beta
&=
\begin{pmatrix}
1&0\\
0&-1
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
0&1\\
1&0
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
0&1\\
-1&0
\end{pmatrix},\\
\beta\alpha
&=
\begin{pmatrix}
0&1\\
1&0
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
1&0\\
0&-1
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
0&-1\\
1&0
\end{pmatrix}.\end{aligned}$ Thus $\alpha\beta=-\beta\alpha,
\qquad
\alpha\beta+\beta\alpha=0.$ Other equivalent matrix choices are possible, but this one makes the component equations especially transparent.

