p.src:
Let $\phi(x,t)$ be a scalar field with its own equation of motion. In the Dirac equation, replace the constant $m$ in the mass term by $g\phi(x,t)$: $i\frac{\partial\Psi}{\partial t}
=
-i\alpha\frac{\partial\Psi}{\partial x}
+g\phi(x,t)\,\beta\Psi.$ The two field equations are then coupled. The fermion acts on the scalar field, and the scalar field acts back on the fermion; considered together, the system is nonlinear even though the Dirac equation is linear in $\Psi$ for a prescribed $\phi$.

